The AbcImport plugin is used to load an Alembic file.
It contains a command (AbcImport) and a node (AlembicNode).
Creation and management of AlembicNode is done via the AbcImport command, and
is only necessary when the scene contains animated data.  Creating and managing
an AlembicNode without the AbcImport command is not currently recommended.

The simplest way to load your Alembic scene is:
AbcImport "scene.abc";

Other options can be viewed via:
AbcImport -h;

Curve UV source names
---------------------
Alembic curves can carry an optional UV source name on the "uv" geometry
parameter. Set it on the curves schema before setting the first sample that
contains UV data:

    Alembic::AbcGeom::OCurves curves( parent, "curves" );
    curves.getSchema().setUVSourceName( "map1" );
    curves.getSchema().set( curvesSample );

The Python binding exposes the same operation:

    curves.getSchema().setUVSourceName( "map1" )

If the UV source name is not set, no sourceName metadata is written and the
file behaves exactly like previous Alembic versions. Calling
setUVSourceName( "" ) explicitly records an empty source name. An importer
must distinguish that case from an unset source name:

    std::string uvSource;
    if ( schema.getUVSourceName( uvSource ) )
    {
        // uvSource is valid and may be empty
    }
    else
    {
        // the source name was never set
    }

In Python, use hasUVSourceName() together with getUVSourceName() to make the
same distinction.

When importing assets, verify the setting after reopening the file. If the
value read back does not match the value that was requested, fail the import
instead of silently accepting incorrect UV assignments.
